WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.155
Consider two events for a shipping address:
version seven sets one address and version eight

00:00:05.155 --> 00:00:10.142
replaces it. A handler can deduplicate repeated
event identifiers and still get the wrong

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:14.625
result if version seven arrives after version
eight with a different identifier.

00:00:14.625 --> 00:00:19.802
Record or compare the entity version when
applying a change, or choose an ordered
processing

00:00:19.802 --> 00:00:24.753
scope that preserves the required sequence.
Idempotency answers whether the same logical

00:00:24.753 --> 00:00:29.873
event produces an extra effect; ordering answers
whether different valid events produce the

00:00:29.873 --> 00:00:30.999
correct final state.

00:00:31.125 --> 00:00:35.800
Draw both the event identifier and entity
version in a worked example. This prevents a

00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.475
common reasoning error where a system is called
reliable simply because duplicates are

00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:44.063
ignored while stale updates can still overwrite
newer information.

00:00:44.125 --> 00:00:49.416
A subscriber receives an event it cannot parse
because a required field has an unexpected

00:00:49.416 --> 00:00:54.885
format. Immediate unlimited retries cannot
repair that message and can crowd out valid
work.

00:00:54.885 --> 00:01:00.295
After a bounded retry policy, move it to a dead-
letter workflow that preserves the payload,

00:01:00.295 --> 00:01:02.613
failure reason, and diagnostic context.

00:01:02.625 --> 00:01:07.691
An operator or repair process can correct the
cause and replay deliberately. Some messages

00:01:07.691 --> 00:01:12.644
should instead expire: an alert that a pickup is
arriving may be useless hours after the

00:01:12.644 --> 00:01:17.653
trip ended. Each subscription needs its own lag
monitoring because analytics may tolerate

00:01:17.653 --> 00:01:19.454
delay that notifications cannot.

00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:24.657
A successful publisher acknowledgement therefore
says little about every downstream result.

00:01:24.657 --> 00:01:29.412
Observe progress and failure at the consumer
responsibility where the useful effect is

00:01:29.412 --> 00:01:30.407
supposed to occur.

00:01:30.625 --> 00:01:35.931
A robot camera produces frames continuously, so
a topic is a natural way to let perception

00:01:35.931 --> 00:01:41.354
and visualization subscribe independently.
Asking for the current map is closer to a
request

00:01:41.354 --> 00:01:46.719
with a result. Asking the robot to navigate to a
destination is a longer goal that benefits

00:01:46.719 --> 00:01:50.669
from progress feedback and cancellation as well
as a final outcome.

00:01:50.669 --> 00:01:55.464
These are different interaction contracts even
if all are implemented with messages

00:01:55.464 --> 00:02:00.779
underneath. Draw the camera stream, map request,
and navigation goal as different connectors

00:02:00.779 --> 00:02:05.458
between nodes. A single generic message arrow
would conceal timing and completion

00:02:05.458 --> 00:02:06.210
expectations.

00:02:06.210 --> 00:02:11.527
Typed interfaces help components evolve
independently, but the architect still has to
choose

00:02:11.527 --> 00:02:14.418
what data and lifecycle each interaction
promises.

00:02:14.418 --> 00:02:19.357
Knowing that a camera node exists does not
establish which frames must be retained, how

00:02:19.357 --> 00:02:24.466
quickly a controller needs them, or what to do
when communication stops. A stale image and

00:02:24.466 --> 00:02:29.688
an emergency-stop command have different
consequences, so their delivery and timing
policies

00:02:29.688 --> 00:02:31.335
should not be copied blindly.

00:02:31.418 --> 00:02:36.701
The centralized registration component in the
historical ROS 1 example illustrates naming

00:02:36.701 --> 00:02:41.628
and discovery; a different version can use a
different discovery architecture while

00:02:41.628 --> 00:02:46.615
preserving the modular node idea. Keep these
concerns separate in the diagram. After

00:02:46.615 --> 00:02:51.423
discovering a peer, a component still needs
compatible interfaces, an appropriate

00:02:51.423 --> 00:02:54.450
communication policy, and defined failure
behavior.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:59.558
For safety-related control, a communication
abstraction alone is insufficient evidence that

00:02:59.558 --> 00:03:03.206
the complete system will meet its timing and
safety requirements.
